I am using ddply (from plyr),
and I have a question.

I have a function which needs 2 matrices as argument

fun(mat1,mat2)

With a dataframe mydf, I want to use ddply in subsets, and the matrices are constructed by cbinding variables on the dataframe

Computing the result for the whole dataframe would be done by

with(mydf,fun(cbind(vara1,vara2),cbind(varb1,varb2)))

I can do this using ddply for groups.

But there is an additional complication:
I want to construct the matrices from many variables, namely 80, and I would rather avoid typing all 80 variable names.

For the whole dataframe, I can do

fun(as.matrix(subset(mydf,select=vara1:vara5)),as.matrix(subset(mydf,select=varb1:varab80)))

is there a way of working of matrices constructed by cbinding variables from the dataframe on the
subsets of dataframes created by the slicing process of ddply?

I am currently doing something like

fun(cbind(vara1,vara2),
      eval(parse(text=paste("cbind(",paste(paste("varb",1:80,","),collapse=","),")"))))

which works but is not really easily readable.

From u2b27719db7267c87 at groups.google.com  Mon Jul 28 21:21:14 2014
